<h2>The claim</h2>
<p>The appellant participated in the combined competitive examination for 2021-22 conducted by the Rajasthan Public Service Commission and was selected in 2024. After obtaining copies of her answer sheets, she waited until May 2025 to file a writ petition seeking re-evaluation of four specific questions — presumably with an eye on merit position and consequential benefits. The Single Judge declined; the Division Bench heard the intra-court special appeal.</p>

<h2>The Bench\'s reasoning</h2>
<p>Two strands decided the appeal. The first is textual: Rule 18 of the Rajasthan State and Subordinate Services (Direct Recruitment by Combined Competitive Examination) Rules, 1999 permits retotalling of marks — an arithmetical check — and nothing more. Re-evaluation, a qualitative reassessment of answers, is simply not a remedy the scheme provides. The second strand is the settled limit on judicial power: courts may exceptionally direct re-evaluation in extraordinary circumstances, but the case must be rare and exceptional, and this was not — four contested questions, raised a year after disclosure of the answer sheets, by a candidate already selected. The appeal was dismissed.</p>
<div class="note"><p>The one-year gap mattered. Examination results underpin selections, appointments and seniority; a challenge that ripens slowly threatens settled third-party positions, and delay is therefore weighed against the challenger even inside the writ jurisdiction where limitation does not formally apply.</p></div>

<h2>The doctrinal map</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h4>Rules first</h4><p>The examination scheme defines the remedies. If it grants retotalling only, re-evaluation cannot be judicially implied into it.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Deference to examiners</h4><p>Assessment of answers is the examiner\'s domain. Courts do not sit as appellate evaluators over marking judgment.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>The narrow exception</h4><p>Interference is reserved for demonstrable error of a kind no reasonable examining body could sustain — the rare and exceptional case.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Promptness</h4><p>Challenges must follow the result or disclosure of answer sheets swiftly; delay is itself a ground to refuse relief.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The appellate dimension</h2>
<p>The case also illustrates the shape of intra-court appeals against discretionary writ refusals. The Division Bench does not ask whether relief could conceivably have been granted; it asks whether the Single Judge\'s exercise of discretion was legally flawed. Where the refusal rests on an accurate reading of the rules and an orthodox application of the restraint doctrine, the special appeal — like Delhi\'s LPA — offers no second discretion. Appellants in such matters must therefore identify a genuine error of principle, not merely renew the merits.</p>
<p>For candidates and public bodies alike, the operational lessons are stable: read the examination rules before litigating; anchor any challenge in a concrete, demonstrable error; move immediately; and treat the intra-court appeal as a forum for legal error, not a rehearing of hope.</p>
<p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice or a solicitation.</p>
